00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.750
<v José Luis><lang es>¡Usted puede recibir una multa!</lang>

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:59.750
<v Orlando>Okay, excellent. Now in Spanish, José Luis, if you have
the sound "ch", how is the word spelled in Spanish?

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:01.800
<v José Luis>CH, CH.

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:06.500
<v Orlando>With a CH, right? Like in the word <lang es>chica</lang>, or <lang es>macho</lang>, <lang es>ocho</lang>.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:12.640
<v Orlando>And so your Spanish ear expects to hear
the sound "ch" when a word is spelled "ch".

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:16.750
<v Orlando>But now we find out that the "ch"
sound is really with a "ti" or a "te".

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:19.500
<v Orlando>And that's why it sounds so different to your Spanish ear.

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:25.000
<v Orlando>Well, I think we should probably go through this
